Conditional structures

conditionals in bash can used in bash using if statement, if-else statement, elif statement with the following syntax

if <condition>;
then <statement>
fi

you can further expand if or then construct so that it takes different sets of actions depending on whether a condition is met.

if <condition>;
then <statment>
else <statement>
fi

elif:

if <condition>;
then <statement>
elif <condition>;
then <statement>
else <statement>
fi

examples:

 if [ 10 -ge 9 ]; then echo "it is correct"; fi

 if [ 10 -ge 11 ]; then echo "it is correct"; else echo "it is wrong"; fi

 if [ -f /etc/resolv.conf ]; then echo "got file"; else echo "didnt got"; fi

 if [ -f /etc/resolv.conf ]; then echo "got file"; elif [ 11 -ge 7 ]; then echo "11>5"; else echo "none of them are true"; fi

 if-else statements rely entirely on the exit status of the command being evaluated.
If the command inside the statement returns an exit status of 0 (Success/True), the then block executes. If it returns any non-zero status (Failure/False), the else block executes.

if [ condition ]; then
    # Executes if exit status is 0 (True)
    echo "Condition met!"
else
    # Executes if exit status is non-zero (False)
    echo "Condition failed!"
fi

Note: The semicolon ; before then is required if they are on the same line. If you put then on its own line, you can omit the semicolon.

You can place an if statement inside another if, elif, or else block to create deeper, multi-layered conditional logic.

if [ condition1 ]; then
    echo "First condition is met."

    # Nested IF statement
    if [ condition2 ]; then
        echo "Both first and second conditions are met."
    fi # Closes the inner loop

fi # Closes the outer loop

TARGET_DIR=chrony
if [[ -d "$TARGET_DIR" ]]; then if [[ -w "$TARGET_DIR" ]]; then echo "target directory exists, it is writable"; else echo "target directory exists, it is now writable"; fi; else echo "target directory does not exists" fi

#!/bin/bash

TARGET_DIR="/var/log/backup"

# Outer condition: Check if the folder exists
if [[ -d "$TARGET_DIR" ]]; then
    echo "Directory exists. Verifying permissions..."

    # Inner (Nested) condition: Check if the folder is writable
    if [[ -w "$TARGET_DIR" ]]; then
        echo "Success: Directory is writable. Running backup now."
        # Backup commands go here...
    else
        echo "Error: Directory exists, but you do not have write permissions."
    fi

else
    echo "Error: Target directory $TARGET_DIR does not exist."
fi


# target directory exists, it is writable

